Mem0 vs Letta vs Zep: Choosing Your AI Agent's Memory Layer
  URL: https://dataaspirant.com/blog/mem0-vs-letta-vs-zep/
  **Mem0** is a memory layer you bolt onto any stack: it extracts facts from conversations and hands them back at prompt time. **Letta** is an agent runtime descended from MemGPT, where the agent itself edits what it remembers using tools. **Zep** stores memory as a temporal knowledge graph that tracks not just facts but when they were true.

## [Letta: the agent that edits its own mind#](https://dataaspirant.com/blog/mem0-vs-letta-vs-zep/#letta-the-agent-that-edits-its-own-mind)
That is also the honest cost: Letta is not a library you sprinkle onto an existing [LangGraph](https://dataaspirant.com/blog/langgraph-tutorial/) app; it is the app.

## [Zep: memory with a time axis#](https://dataaspirant.com/blog/mem0-vs-letta-vs-zep/#zep-memory-with-a-time-axis)
![Zep Graphiti bi-temporal memory diagram showing world time and system time for a user moving from London to Berlin, with valid_at and invalid_at timestamps](https://d2565bmq0op2ju.cloudfront.net/blog-assets/mem0-vs-letta-vs-zep-a59feb/images/zep-graphiti-bitemporal-agent-memory-exp-fa2a9922.png)Graphiti tracks both when a fact was true in the world and when the system learned or superseded it, allowing historical and current answers to coexist.

Zep's engine, the open-source **Graphiti** (one of the most-starred agent-memory projects on GitHub), ingests **episodes**: a conversation turn, a JSON record, a document.

The distinctive machinery is **bi-temporal modelling**: every fact carries two timelines, one for the world and one for the system, using the four timestamps in the table below.

When new information contradicts an existing edge, Graphiti does not delete it; it writes an `invalid_at`, a mechanism called edge invalidation.

Zep's own 2025 paper reports beating MemGPT on the Deep Memory Retrieval benchmark (94.8% versus 93.4%) and larger gains on LongMemEval's temporal reasoning tasks alongside roughly 90% lower latency than full-context baselines.

Graphiti's bi-temporal model: two timelines per fact, four timestamps. The first pair describes the world; the second pair describes the system's knowledge of it.

## [Hands-on: the same memory job, three ways#](https://dataaspirant.com/blog/mem0-vs-letta-vs-zep/#hands-on-the-same-memory-job-three-ways)
Install with `pip install mem0ai letta-client graphiti-core` (Graphiti additionally wants a Neo4j or FalkorDB instance; Zep's hosted platform wraps it as an API).

One scenario, three philosophies: Mem0 consolidates facts, Letta lets the agent edit its own blocks, Graphiti closes the old fact with a timestamp.

## [Head to head#](https://dataaspirant.com/blog/mem0-vs-letta-vs-zep/#head-to-head)
|  | Mem0 | Letta | Zep (Graphiti) |
|-|-|-|-|
| What it is | Memory API beside your agent | Agent runtime with self-editing memory | Temporal knowledge graph service |
| Memory unit | Distilled facts, scoped by user, agent or run | Editable in-context blocks plus recall and archival tiers | Entities and relationship edges with validity intervals |
| Handles a changed fact by | Consolidation: UPDATE to current state | Agent rewrites its own block | Edge invalidation: old fact closed with a timestamp, kept queryable |
| Temporal reasoning | Current-state focus | Whatever the agent wrote down | First-class: bi-temporal, point-in-time queries |
| Integration effort | Hours: two API calls and a prompt paste | Days to weeks: adopt the runtime and server | Hours against hosted Zep; days self-hosting Graphiti plus a graph database |

## Frequently asked questions
### What is the main difference between Mem0, Letta and Zep?
They occupy different architectural layers. Mem0 is a memory API beside your agent: it extracts and consolidates facts from conversations and serves them at prompt time. Letta is an agent runtime where the model edits its own in-context memory blocks with tools, descended from MemGPT. Zep stores memory as a temporal knowledge graph (via its open-source engine Graphiti) where facts carry validity intervals and contradictions close old facts instead of deleting them.

